<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 796 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ubi caritas</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="la">Ubi caritas et amor</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin chant from about 796, sung on the Thursday before Easter, saying that where there is true love between people, God is there.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>attributed to Paulinus of Aquileia</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (Gregorian chant)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Western (Latin) Church</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>796 CE (attributed)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (Latin text attributed to Paulinus of Aquileia (796 CE); chant melody probably late 8th century)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubi-caritas">Ubi caritas</a>&quot; is a hymn of the Western Church whose text is attributed to Paulinus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> in 796 CE; its traditional melody probably also comes from the late 8th century. For a long time it was one of the antiphons sung at the washing of feet on Maundy Thursday, part of the Holy Thursday evening liturgy, and it is now and then sung at Eucharistic Adoration and at Benediction of the Blessed Sacrament. The Roman Missal of 1970 (third typical edition 2002) moved it from the foot-washing rite, the mandatum, to the offertory procession of the Holy Thursday evening Mass of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Lord's Supper</a>. In the second typical edition of 1975 the response was altered, after certain very early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, to &quot;Ubi caritas est vera, Deus ibi est&quot;: &quot;Where true charity is, God is there&quot;. The hymn is also found in Anglican and Lutheran hymnals.</p><p>Modern composers have set it again and again. Maurice Duruflé used the Gregorian melody, with the refrain and the first stanza, as the first of his Quatre Motets sur des thèmes grégoriens. In 1960 an English translation, &quot;Where Charity and Love Prevail&quot;, was copyrighted, to a tune Dom Paul Benoit adapted from the chant of Veni redemptor gentium. Jacques Berthier's Taizé chant of 1978 keeps only the refrain, with verses from 1 Corinthians 13. Later choral settings include those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Conte, Stephen DeCesare, Ola Gjeilo and Paul Mealor, whose setting was sung at the wedding of Prince William and Catherine Middleton in 2011.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The refrain declares that God is present where charity and love are; since 1975 the Roman Missal reads &quot;Where true charity is, God is there&quot;. Sung for centuries while feet were washed on Maundy Thursday, it is a calm and earnest chant of love among the faithful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ubi cáritas et amor, Deus ibi est.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where charity and love are, God is there.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Congregávit nos in unum Christi amor.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The love of Christ has gathered us into one.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Exsultémus et in ipso iucundémur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us rejoice and be glad in Him.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Timeámus et amémus Deum vivum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us fear and love the living God.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et ex corde diligámus nos sincéro.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And from a sincere heart let us love one another.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ubi cáritas et amor, Deus ibi est.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where charity and love are, God is there.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Simul ergo cum in unum congregámur:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Therefore, when we are gathered together as one:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ne nos mente dividámur, caveámus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us take care that we be not divided in mind.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Cessent iúrgia malígna, cessent lites.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let malicious quarrels cease, let strife cease.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et in médio nostri sit Christus Deus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And in our midst may Christ God be.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ubi cáritas et amor, Deus ibi est.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where charity and love are, God is there.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Simul quoque cum beátis videámus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Also, together with the blessed, may we see</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gloriánter vultum tuum, Christe Deus:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Gloriously Your face, O Christ God:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gáudium, quod est imménsum atque probum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A joy which is immense and pure.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sæcula per infiníta sæculórum. Amen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For endless ages of ages. Amen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Attributed to Paulinus of Aquileia (796); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (English Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubi_caritas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: English translation as given in Wikipedia's article (CC BY-SA). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubi_caritas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
